  "read": {
    "headline": "Oil is back above $107, and the front end is not following it.",
    "standfirst": "Friday was the relief day: Iran offered to reopen Hormuz within seven days, Brent settled 2.1% lower, the ten-year came back from a 5.228% high and the S&P 500 rose 0.51% to 7,743.41 to close its first winning week in three. Then Trump rejected the offer on Saturday, and by 05:44 UTC on Monday Brent is $107.58, up 3.12%, WTI $94.66, gold $4,216 and down 2.43%, the Kospi off 2.50% and the dollar at a two-month high. The two-year, at 4.81%, fell on Friday and is lower on the week even as the thirty-year rose — October's hike is a little less priced, duration a little more punished. The board is Friday's New York close for US assets and live for everything else.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "Brent recovered Friday's fall before New York woke up.",
        "text": "Brent is $107.58, up 3.12% on the day and 4.37% over five days, 22.11% over a month and 76.01% on the year; WTI is $94.66, up 2.43% and 2.71% on the week now that last week's roll distortion has dropped out of the five-day column. Heating oil rose 3.07% and is up 115.56% year to date, the largest number on the board. Gasoline is the odd one out, down 10.14% over five days after the diesel export ban was floated and withdrawn. The energy sector fell 0.89% on Friday and 0.53% on the week on the oil dip it is now unwinding; natural gas, down 3.01% on the day, is the only energy contract that did not price the weekend."
      },
      {
        "title": "The front end came off. The long end did not.",
        "text": "The two-year fell to 4.81% on Friday and is down on the week; the ten-year at 5.17% is up 1.17% over five days; the thirty-year at 5.49% rose 0.37% on the day and 1.67% on the week; the twenty-year at 5.54% is the highest point on the curve. Two-tens is 36bp from 31bp on Thursday. In the ETFs that is the 1–3 year fund up 0.11% on the week while the 20-year-plus fund lost 1.42% and is down 8.99% on the year; municipals fell 0.85% over five days, the worst of the bond rows. The front end is pricing October at about 65% instead of 71%. The long end is pricing supply, term premium and an inflation-expectations survey at 4.6% — and none of those changed over the weekend."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold at $4,216 is the lowest since early August. Silver fell twice as fast.",
        "text": "Gold lost 2.43% on the day and 2.36% over five days, is down 6.92% over a month and 3.89% on the year; silver fell 4.27% to $62.03 and is off 14.79% year to date; platinum dropped 3.13%. A weekend in which the oil-supply headline went the wrong way was, on paper, a weekend for a hedge, and the metals sold instead, because a yield of 5.17% on a ten-year Treasury is the price of holding something that pays nothing. Copper fell 1.59% but is up 21.00% over six months and 15.90% on the year, still the one metal that has worked, as an industrial input rather than a store of value."
      },
      {
        "title": "Korea: up 2.55% on Friday in New York, down 2.50% on Monday in Seoul.",
        "text": "The Korea ETF rose 2.55% on Friday and is up 54.72% over six months and 92.53% on the year; the Kospi itself is down 2.50% this morning, its first session after the Chuseok holiday, with the two big chipmakers off more than 4%. Taiwan is up 1.51% and 80.67% year to date; Japan rose 2.21% on Friday and the Nikkei is flat this morning. India rose 0.63% on Friday but is down 3.43% over a month, and the Nifty is 1.37% lower today below 23,000 with a three-day bank strike starting. China fell 0.38% and is off 4.73% over a month; the CSI 300 is down about 2% this morning. The country column is Friday's dollar close; Asia has already voted on the weekend, and it voted against."
      },
      {
        "title": "The sector week: health care up 1.13%, utilities down 0.60%, and a Friday led by industrials.",
        "text": "Over five days health care rose 1.13%, financials 0.55% and technology 0.48%, while materials fell 0.95%, real estate 0.67% and utilities 0.60%; the S&P 500 ETF gained 0.46%. Friday's leaders were industrials at 0.95% and technology at 0.80%; energy fell 0.89% and communication services 0.90%. Utilities remain the worst sector over three months at −14.15% and on the year at −7.45%; financials at +0.13% year to date are barely positive. Among the factors, private equity beat the index by 0.80% on Friday but trails it by 27.35% on the year; small-cap growth lagged by 0.57% and small caps trail by 10.25% over three months. The VIX closed at 14.87, down 5.11%, on a day the thirty-year Treasury set a 22-year high."
      },
      {
        "title": "The dollar is at a two-month high; the yen is the one currency fighting it.",
        "text": "The euro is down 1.72% over a month and 3.17% on the year; sterling is off 2.15% over a month, near a three-month low after Bailey said high energy prices make the Bank's hold harder to keep. USD/JPY is 157.75, up 0.36% today but down 0.34% on the week after Japan's finance minister said Trump had raised the weak yen with the prime minister and the pair fell to 156.94 on Friday. The dollar gained 0.61% on the franc and 1.31% on the peso over five days; the peso is 4.25% weaker over a month. The rupee at 95.95 is 6.68% weaker on the year, the lira 13.92%. In crypto, Bitcoin at $83,093 is down 1.62% and 6.36% year to date; Litecoin's 15.09% five-day gain and 45.21% month are still the largest moves anywhere on the board, and Chainlink at +9.64% is the only major coin positive on the year."
      }
    ]
  },
